From bce7bfc08803ee516083d15572d51715d4b4e698 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 11:44:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] codegen_c: a volatile load or store of a ZST emits nothing

sha1 0.11.0 (arti 2.8.0) libsha1.rlib.c `BlockBuffer<U64, Eager>::zeroize`:
`*(volatile tUNIT *)var4 = var5;` -> gcc `'var5' undeclared`.

zeroize 1.8.2's blanket `impl<Z: DefaultIsZeroes> Zeroize for Z` writes
`Z::default()` with `ptr::write_volatile`, and `()` is in its
DefaultIsZeroes list. block-buffer 0.12's `Eager` kind keeps the position
in the buffer's last byte and has `type Pos = ()`, so `BlockBuffer::zeroize`
(digest's `zeroize` feature, which arti enables) volatile-writes a `()`.
The C backend emitted volatile_load / volatile_store / nontemporal_store
for every type parameter, naming the value local it never declares for
a ZST (disallow_empty_structs). A volatile access of a ZST has nothing to
touch: the three intrinsics now emit a `/* zst */` placeholder, as
transmute already does.

New, not a regression: sha1 0.10.x's block-buffer 0.10 has `pos: u8`, and
a ZST volatile access never compiled in the C backend.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5.1
